---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { site, skills } from '../site.config';
---

<Base title="About" description={`About ${site.name}.`}>
  <PageHeader label="About" title="A bit <em>about me</em>." />
  <div class="container-page grid gap-14 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_18rem]">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I’m a backend and platform engineer. I like the unglamorous middle of a system: the content API, the event bus,
        the CDN rules, the release pipeline. When those work, editors publish in seconds and nobody thinks about them.
      </p>
      <p>
        I came to engineering sideways. I studied English at Guilford College, spent ten years teaching technology
        and Spanish, and then built front ends for NPR's distribution system. Teaching still shapes how I work: I explain things plainly and I
        write them down.
      </p>
      <p>
        I then spent twelve years at Turner, WarnerMedia, and Warner Bros. Discovery, the same company under three
        names. I started on the front end of NBA.com and the NBA League Pass storefront, moved to the site and API for
        Turner Classic Movies, and then to the shared publishing platform behind HBO.com, TCM.com, DC.com, and other
        brand sites.
      </p>
      <p>
        On that platform my work shifted from building features to designing how the pieces fit. I designed its Kafka
        event bus, led the backend side of moving TCM.com onto it, and designed a publishing path that replaces a
        whole-site rebuild with a single JSON manifest. Along the way I wrote ten architecture decision records and
        reviewed more than 1,700 pull requests.
      </p>
      <p>
        Runbooks, migration plans, and developer guides are part of how I ship, because a system
        that only one person understands is a liability. I also take incidents seriously: I’ve written the post-incident
        reports and then filed and driven the hardening work that follows.
      </p>
      <p>
        Lately I’ve been working with AI coding tools every day, and I built an agent pipeline that
        triages and drafts fixes for vulnerability tickets while leaving the merge decision to a person.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’m looking for a backend or platform engineering role where the problems are architectural and the team cares
        about operating what it builds.
      </p>
      {/* TODO: add a line about life outside work so there is a person on the page. */}
      <p>
        The fastest way to reach me is <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="space-y-8">
      {
        skills.map((group) => (
          <div class="border-t border-line pt-4">
            <h2 class="label">{group.group}</h2>
            <ul class="mt-3 space-y-1.5">
              {group.items.map((item) => (
                <li>{item}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </aside>
  </div>
</Base>
